<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Henrik Ibsen</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1828">1828</a>–<a href="/years/1906">1906</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Henrik Ibsen (1828–1906) was a Norwegian playwright, often called the father of modern drama. After early verse dramas such as Brand and Peer Gynt, written in Italian exile, he turned to realistic prose plays that examined the morals of middle-class society, among them A Doll's House (1879), Ghosts, An Enemy of the People and Hedda Gabler. He is among the most performed dramatists in the world.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/henrik-ibsen.jpg" alt="Henrik Ibsen" width="320" height="391" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Eilif Peterssen · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Henrik_Ibsen_av_Eilif_Peterssen_1895.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Henrik Johan Ibsen was born on 20 March 1828 into an affluent merchant family in the port town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/skien">Skien</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telemark">Telemark</a>. His father, Knud Plesner Ibsen, was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a> and luxury goods merchant, and his mother, Marichen Cornelia Martine Altenburg, belonged like him to the extended Paus family, which had held wealth and power in Telemark since the mid-1500s. Older biographies pictured a descent from privilege into hardship, with a failed and tyrannical father, but a later reading finds this romanticised. At fifteen Ibsen left school and became an apprentice pharmacist in Grimstad, where he began writing plays. His first play, the tragedy Catilina, was published in 1850 under a pseudonym but not performed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Ibsen established himself as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a> director in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> during the 1850s, though the many plays he wrote in those years were unsuccessful. In 1864 he left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oslo">Christiania</a> for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sorrento">Sorrento</a> and spent the next 27 years in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, mostly in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dresden">Dresden</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich">Munich</a>, before returning to Christiania in 1891. Brand and Peer Gynt, the second with incidental music by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edvard-grieg">Edvard Grieg</a>, brought him critical acclaim and some financial success. After Peer Gynt he abandoned verse for realistic prose, and he came to treat what he called the drama of ideas.</p><p>His plays from A Doll's House of 1879 onward, among them Ghosts, An Enemy of the People, The Wild Duck, Rosmersholm and Hedda Gabler, examined the morals and family life of the middle class and caused an uproar in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a>, across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and in America. Many of them are set in Norwegian bourgeois surroundings resembling <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/skien">Skien</a>, and he took names and characters from his own relatives. Critics often rate The Wild Duck and Rosmersholm his best work, while Ibsen himself regarded Emperor and Galilean as his masterpiece.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Ibsen died at his home in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oslo">Kristiania</a> on 23 May 1906, after a series of strokes that began in March 1900. When his nurse told a visitor on the previous day that he was a little better, his last word was the reply Tvertimod, meaning On the contrary. He was buried in Vår Frelsers gravlund in Oslo. By 2014 he was the most frequently performed dramatist in the world after Shakespeare, and he influenced writers such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-bernard-shaw">George Bernard Shaw</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oscar-wilde">Oscar Wilde</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-joyce">James Joyce</a>. His son Sigurd Ibsen became prime minister of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Henrik Ibsen” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Modern Norwegian national identity was built on this medieval past and on nineteenth-century culture: <b>Henrik Ibsen</b>, Edvard Grieg and Edvard Munch won international fame, and Fridtjof Nansen and Roald Amundsen led polar exploration, Amundsen reaching the South Pole in 1911.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-norway">Kingdom of Norway · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Stockmanngården. Died in Christiania. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;The majority never has right on its side. Never, I say! That is one of these social lies against which an independent, intelligent man must wage war.&#8221;</p><footer>An Enemy of the People (1882), act 4 &#183; tr. R. Farquharson Sharp, 1911 &#183; spoken by Dr Stockmann &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/2446/pg2446-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;…the strongest man in the world is he who stands most alone.&#8221;</p><footer>An Enemy of the People (1882), act 5 &#183; tr. R. Farquharson Sharp, 1911 &#183; spoken by Dr Stockmann &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/2446/pg2446-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;I believe that before all else I am a reasonable human being, just as you are—or, at all events, that I must try and become one.&#8221;</p><footer>A Doll's House (1879), act 3 &#183; tr. R. Farquharson Sharp &#183; spoken by Nora &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/2542/pg2542-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><details class="enc-q-more"><summary>3 more</summary><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;But I almost think we are all of us ghosts, Pastor Manders. It is not only what we have inherited from our father and mother that &quot;walks&quot; in us. It is all sorts of dead ideas, and lifeless old beliefs, and so forth.&#8221;</p><footer>Ghosts (1881), act 2 &#183; tr. William Archer &#183; spoken by Mrs Alving &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/8121/pg8121-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;…the spirit of truth and the spirit of freedom--they are the pillars of society.&#8221;</p><footer>Pillars of Society (1877), closing words &#183; tr. R. Farquharson Sharp &#183; spoken by Lona Hessel &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/2296/pg2296-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;But our home has been nothing but a playroom. I have been your doll-wife, just as at home I was papa’s doll-child; and here the children have been my dolls.&#8221;</p><footer>A Doll's House (1879), act 3 &#183; tr. R. Farquharson Sharp &#183; spoken by Nora &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/2542/pg2542-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote></details><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 595 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
